From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CBOC-Vita <344212538+CBOC-Vita@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 23:08:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 70/70] gl: a cheaper FXAA pass (5 taps for a flat pixel, 7 for
 an edge pixel, against 9 for every pixel)

gl_fxaa_quality=fast (the default for gfx.antialiasing=fxaa) replaces the nine-tap pass; =high is the first version, kept
for A/B. The decision is the same (luma of the pixel and its four diagonal neighbours, the same edge test); what changed
is how it is read:

* a vertex stage of its own gives the five tap coordinates as varyings, so none of the five is a dependent read;
* the edge test is made right after them, and a pixel off an edge (about 80 % of a mission frame's pixels) is written back
  unchanged, exactly as before;
* an edge pixel takes two blur taps along the edge (at 0.33 of the direction, the width that matched the old four-tap
  average best on mission-1 frames) instead of four, kept inside the per-channel range of the five taps instead of
  choosing between two blur widths by a third luma test;
* the colour arithmetic is mediump; coordinates stay at full precision.

If the cheaper program does not build the first is used. ge_replay --shader-lint covers both (the new pass has three
varying slots).

Mission-1 dumps, 50 frames, software GL: the new pass differs from the old FXAA by 0.115 of 255 on average (PSNR 47.8 dB,
worst frame 45.6), 8.5 % of pixels differ and 0.3 % by more than 8 levels; the old pass differs from no FXAA by 0.42
(10 % of pixels). Looked at crops of edges beside each other. Not measured on a Vita: no Vita shader compiler or device was
used, so the tap and operation counts are from the source.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
